@namespace Estimation.Components.DepartmentPlanning

<MudDialog>
    <TitleContent>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">How to read Department Planning</MudText>
    </TitleContent>
    <DialogContent>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold mt-1">One grid, four levels</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            Strategic objectives hold portfolio epics, portfolio epics hold business outcomes, and business outcomes hold features,
            the pieces of work the teams deliver. Every number on a higher row adds up the features underneath it.
            Open or close a row with the arrow in front of its name, or double-click the row. <b>Open to</b> chooses the level the rows start at;
            the levels above it become columns, so Open to F lists every feature with its objective, epic and outcome next to it.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Demand, capacity and the line</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            <b>Demand</b> is the work asked of the teams for the planning period, in story points.
            <b>Capacity</b> is what the teams can deliver in that period, after holidays and part-time coefficients, in story points at each team's story points per day;
            switch on <b>Include IP sprint</b> to count the innovation and planning sprint as well, as on the PI Capacity page.
            Each ART ranks its portfolio epics or business outcomes; the teams fill their capacity in that order.
            Work above every line is <b>planned</b>. Work below a team line does not fit the team's capacity, and work below an ART line
            gets no capacity from its ART: both are <b>not planned</b>.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Hierarchy or capacity drawdown</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The switch above the Objectives grid chooses how its rows are built.
            <b>Hierarchy</b> starts from what the department wants to achieve and shows how much of the capacity each objective takes.
            <b>Capacity drawdown</b> starts from each ART, with its capacity next to its name and on its line: its items in ranking order,
            the <b>Drawdown</b> column with the running total of planned story points, the point where the capacity runs out, and the line.
            Team columns show the same drawdown per team. Filters and the search hide rows but never change the drawdown:
            it always counts every feature on the team boards, so a visible item keeps its real place against the capacity.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Signals</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            <b>RAG</b> is the delivery confidence reported in Jira; a small second dot shows the worst RAG underneath.
            <b>Dependencies at risk</b> are links between features that are planned out of order or late.
            <b>Features at Risk</b> are open features with a red RAG, a blocked status or a dependency at risk.
            The <b>Features Hygiene issues</b> tile and the <b>Hygiene issues</b> column count features that fail an ART hygiene rule, for example missing acceptance criteria.
            <b>Progress</b> is the share of features that are done; Rejected and Cancelled count as done, and a done feature is never at risk.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Six ways to look at the same numbers</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            The buttons at the top right switch the picture; the department, planning increment, ARTs, filters and search stay the same.
            <b>Objectives</b> is the grid with every level and exact numbers, and the only picture with the Hierarchy and Capacity drawdown
            switch, Open to and Columns. <b>Timeline</b> lays epics and outcomes on a calendar against the planning increment
            and today. Both have <b>Full screen</b> to fill the screen with them; Esc returns. <b>Treemap</b> shows how much of
            the planning increment each objective, epic and outcome takes, as area. <b>Waterline</b> fills one column per
            ART in ranking order, so what pokes out above the capacity line does not fit. <b>Risk quadrant</b> places every
            portfolio epic, or business outcome, by size and risk, and lists the top-right quadrant, the short list for a decision,
            beside the chart; click another quadrant's name to list that one. <b>Flow</b> follows the planned story points
            from the objectives into the ARTs that deliver them, with what is not planned flowing to a separate outlet.
            The Waterline always shows the capacity drawdown and the other pictures the hierarchy.
            In the pictures a click on an item opens its details. In the Treemap a click first zooms into an item that holds smaller ones:
            Ctrl+click or Shift+click opens its details, and Backspace or the path above the tiles zooms back out. In the Timeline a click
            selects a row, a double-click folds it and the details button opens the details.
            The zoom buttons scale the Objectives grid, Treemap, Waterline, Risk quadrant and Flow, and each view remembers its own scale.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Working with the grid</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
            Click a row to select it; the details button in the Actions column, or Enter, opens its details, with links to the pages where the work is managed.
            Click a column header to sort, and again to reverse the order: rows are sorted among the rows under the same parent, and when
            Open to starts below the top level the top rows are sorted as one list. Rank follows the ART rankings: an objective takes the best
            rank of its epics, an epic whose ART ranks outcomes the best rank of its outcomes, and in the one list a row without a rank of its own
            takes the rank of its outcome or epic. Rows without any ART rank come after the ranked ones, in team board order. In the capacity
            drawdown Rank keeps each ART's ranking order, and opened to ART or PE/BO the ARTs and their ranked items keep their order whatever the column.
            Use the zoom buttons for a denser or larger grid, <b>Full screen</b> to show only the grid (Esc returns) and <b>Columns</b> to choose the columns.
            The search box narrows every picture to the items whose key or title matches (in the capacity drawdown also the ART names),
            with their parents and everything under them. The Not planned, Progress, Features at Risk and Features Hygiene issues tiles
            and the RAG chips work as quick filters, and <b>Show empty</b> also lists objectives, epics and outcomes
            without features in this planning increment.
            Keyboard: up and down arrows move, right opens a row, left closes it or goes to its parent, Enter opens the details, plus and minus
            open and close, Shift with an arrow scrolls the grid or the Waterline; in the Waterline the arrows move between items and ARTs.
            <b>One column per team</b> in Columns adds each team's planned story points, with the team's capacity in its header.
            Export writes the rows the search and filters leave to Excel, fully expanded whatever the Open to level and columns, with one
            column per level and a Total row that counts each feature once; from the other pictures it writes the grid behind them.
            In the capacity drawdown it also writes the ART, the drawdown, where capacity runs out and each ART line. With One column per team on, each team gets its own columns.
            The address in the browser keeps the department, planning increment, ARTs, picture, Hierarchy or Capacity drawdown, Open to level and Include IP sprint,
            so a copied link opens the same page; the search, filters and sorting are not in it.
        </MudText>

        <MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle2" Class="font-weight-bold">Which features are counted</MudText>
        <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">
            The same ones as on the team capacity boards: features planned on the department's teams for the chosen planning period,
            including those matched by the period's labels and those added to a board by hand. A feature shared by several teams or
            strategic objectives is counted once in the totals.
            The ARTs box, shown when the department has more than one ART, narrows the page, capacity included, to the teams of the chosen ARTs.
        </MudText>
    </DialogContent>
    <DialogActions>
        <MudButton Color="Color.Primary" OnClick="@(() => MudDialog.Close())">Close</MudButton>
    </DialogActions>
</MudDialog>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter] private IMudDialogInstance MudDialog { get; set; } = null!;
}
